ssumptions, keep experiment scripts in ./experiments.\\n   - When an experiment demonstrates a real-world use case of the software, add it to ./examples.\\n   - When you face something extremely hard, use divide and conquer.\\nInitial research.\\n   - When you start, create a detailed plan for yourself and follow your todo list step by step. Add as many relevant points from these guidelines to the todo list as practical so you can track the work clearly.\\n   - When you read issue, read all details and comments thoroughly.\\n   - When you see screenshots or images in issue descriptions, pull request descriptions, comments, or discussions, download the image to a local file first, then use the Read tool to view and analyze it. Before reading downloaded images with the Read tool, verify that the file is a valid image rather than HTML by using a CLI tool such as the 'file' command. When the file command shows \\\"HTML\\\", \\\"text\\\", or \\\"ASCII text\\\", the download failed, so do not call Read on that file. When images are from GitHub issues or PRs, such as URLs containing \\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\", use: curl -L -H \\\"Authorization: [REDACTED] $(gh auth token)\\\" -o <filename> \\\"<url>\\\"\\n   - When you need issue details, use gh issue view https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1328.\\n   - When you need related code, use gh search code --owner link-assistant [keywords].\\n   - When you need repo context, read files in your working directory.\\n   - When you study related work, study the most recent related pull requests.\\n   - When the issue is not defined clearly enough, write a comment with clarifying questions.\\n   - When accessing GitHub Gists, use gh gist view command instead of direct URL fetching.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug, find the actual root cause first and run as many experiments as needed.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug and the code does not have enough tracing or logs, add them and keep them in the code with the default state switched off.\\n   - When you need comments on a pull request, note that GitHub has three different comment types with different API endpoints:\\n      1. PR review comments (inline code comments): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/1329/comments --paginate\\n      2. PR conversation comments (general discussion): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1329/comments --paginate\\n      3. PR reviews (approve/request changes): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/1329/reviews --paginate\\n      Note: The command \\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\" only returns conversation comments and misses review comments.\\n   - When you need the latest comments on the issue, use gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1328/comments --paginate.\\nSolution development and testing.\\n   - When issue is solvable, first create a test that reproduces the problem, then implement the fix.\\n   - When coding, commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request branch so interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n   - When you test:\\n      start from testing of small functions using separate scripts;\\n      write unit tests with mocks for easy and quick start.\\n   - When you test integrations, use existing framework.\\n   - When you test solution draft, include automated checks in pr.\\n   - When you write or modify tests, consider setting reasonable timeouts at test, suite, and CI job levels so failures surface quickly instead of hanging.\\n   - When you see repeated test timeout patterns in CI, investigate the root cause rather than increasing timeouts.\\n   - When the issue is unclear, write a comment on the issue with questions.\\n   - When you encounter any problems that you are unable to solve yourself, write a comment to the pull request asking for help.\\n   - When you need human help, use gh pr comment 1329 --body \\\"your message\\\" to comment on existing PR.\\nReproducible testing.\\n   - When fixing a bug, create a test that reproduces the problem before implementing the fix. When you cannot reproduce the problem, you cannot verify the fix.\\n   - When encountering logic bugs, write an automated test that fails due to the bug, then implement the fix to make it pass.\\n   - When encountering UI bugs, capture a screenshot showing the problem state, then create a visual regression test or manual verification screenshot after the fix.\\n   - When creating tests, prefer minimum reproducible examples, meaning the simplest test case that demonstrates the issue.\\n   - When submitting a fix, include in the PR description: (1) how to reproduce the issue, (2) the automated test that verifies the fix, (3) before/after screenshots for UI issues.\\n   - When a bug fix does not have a reproducing test, treat the fix as incomplete because regressions can occur later without notice.\\nPreparing pull request.\\n   - When you code, follow contributing guidelines.\\n   - When you commit, write clear message.\\n   - When you need examples of style, use gh pr list --repo link-assistant/formal-ai --state merged --search [keywords].\\n   - When you open pr, describe solution draft and include tests.\\n   - When there is a package with version and GitHub Actions workflows for automatic release, update the version in your pull request to prepare for next release.\\n   - When you update existing pr 1329, use gh pr edit to modify title and description.\\n   - When you finalize the pull request:\\n      check that the pull request title and description are updated (the PR may start with a [WIP] prefix and a placeholder description that should be replaced with the actual title and description of the changes),\\n      follow style from merged prs for code, title, and description,\\n      check that no uncommitted changes corresponding to the original requirements are left behind,\\n      check that the default branch is merged into the pull request branch,\\n      check that all CI checks are passing if they exist before you finish, including checks that already fail on the default branch (when a check needs a human action, make CI on the pull request pass without it and CI on the default branch fail with an explicit message saying what to do),\\n      check for latest comments on the issue and pull request to ensure no recent feedback was missed,\\n      double-check that all changes in the pull request address the original requirements of the issue,\\n      check for newly introduced bugs in the pull request by carefully reading gh pr diff,\\n      check that no previously existing features were removed without an explicit request in the issue description, issue comments, or pull request comments.\\n   - When you finish implementation, use gh pr ready 1329.\\nWorkflow and collaboration.\\n   - When you check branch, verify with git branch --show-current.\\n   - When you push, push only to branch issue-1328-c97c3f0fa146.\\n   - When you finish, create a pull request from branch issue-1328-c97c3f0fa146.\\n   - When pr 1329 already exists for this branch, update it instead of creating new one.\\n   - When you organize workflow, use pull requests instead of direct merges to default branch (main or master).\\n   - When you manage commits, preserve commit history for later analysis.\\n   - When you contribute, keep repository history forward-moving with regular commits, pushes, and reverts if needed.\\n   - When you face conflict that you cannot resolve yourself, ask for help.\\n   - When you collaborate, respect branch protections by working only on issue-1328-c97c3f0fa146.\\n   - When you mention a result, include the pull request URL or comment URL.\\n   - When you need to create pr, remember pr 1329 already exists for this branch.\\nSelf review.\\n   - When you check your solution draft, run all tests locally.\\n   - When you check your solution draft, verify git status shows a clean working tree with no uncommitted changes.\\n   - When you compare with repo style, use gh pr diff [number].\\n   - When you finalize, confirm code, tests, and description are consistent.\\nGitHub CLI command patterns.\\n   - When fetching lists from GitHub API, use the --paginate flag to ensure all results are returned (GitHub returns max 30 per page by default).\\n   - When listing PR review comments (inline code comments), use gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/NUMBER/comments --paginate.\\n   - When listing PR conversation comments, use gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER/comments --paginate.\\n   - When listing PR reviews, use gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/NUMBER/reviews --paginate.\\n   - When listing issue comments, use gh api repos/OWNER/REPO/issues/NUMBER/comments --paginate.\\n   - When adding PR comment, use gh pr comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When adding issue comment, use gh issue comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When viewing PR details, use gh pr view NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When filtering with jq, use gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/1329/comments --paginate --jq 'reverse | .[0:5]'.\\nPlaywright MCP usage (browser automation via MCP tools).\\n   - When you develop frontend web applications (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Vue, Angular, etc.), use Playwright MCP tools to test the UI in a real browser.\\n   - When WebFetch tool fails to retrieve expected content (e.g., returns empty content, JavaScript-rendered pages, or login-protected pages), use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for web browsing.\\n   - When WebSearch tool fails or returns insufficient results, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for internet search.\\n   - When you need to interact with dynamic web pages that require JavaScript execution, use Playwright MCP tools.\\n   - When you need to visually verify how a web page looks or take screenshots, use browser_take_screenshot from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you need to fill forms, click buttons, or perform user interactions on web pages, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_click, browser_type, browser_fill_form).\\n   - When you need to test responsive design or different viewport sizes, use browser_resize from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you finish using the browser, close it with browser_close to free resources.\\n   - When reproducing UI bugs, use browser_take_screenshot to capture the problem state before implementing any fix.\\n   - When fixing UI bugs, take before/after screenshots to provide visual evidence of the fix for human verification.\\n   - When creating UI tests, save baseline screenshots to the repository for visual regression testing.\\n   - When verifying UI fixes, compare screenshots to ensure the fix does not introduce unintended visual changes.\\nIssue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1328\\nYour prepared branch: issue-1328-c97c3f0fa146\\nYour prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1792503390059\\nYour prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/1329\\nCollect data related about the issue to this repository, make sure we compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/1328/pulls/1329 folder.\\nProceed.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_dad879a774bd9297\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1328' --json title --jq .title && echo && gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1328' --json body --jq .body; printf '\\\\n__formal_ai_exit=%s\\\\n' $?\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_dad879a774bd9297\",\"content\":\"0.354.1 loops for 4.5 h at 100% CPU via Codex: pushes to branch 'so' (a word from the prompt), then calls web_run with {} 119 times (wrong 'query' key, no repeat stop)\\n\\n## What happened\\n\\nOn 2026-10-20 a Hive Mind run, `solve https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/issues/1 --tool codex --model formal-ai`, kept the Formal AI sidecar at **100% of one CPU core for more than 4.5 hours** and produced nothing. It is still running; it was not stopped, so the loop can be watched live.\\n\\nFormal AI did the real work in the first 13 minutes, then spent hours doing nothing useful:\\n\\n| Time (UTC) | Codex item | What Formal AI did | Result |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 08:29 | item_1 | `gh issue view … --json title`, `… --json body` | ok |\\n| 08:31–08:33 | item_3, item_5 | added `main.rs`, `tests/verify-output.sh` | ok |\\n| 08:35 | item_7 | `rustc main.rs -o main` | exit 0 |\\n| 08:37 | item_9 | `sh tests/verify-output.sh` | `Hello, World!` |\\n| 08:41:51 | item_11 | `git add … && git commit … && git push -q origin 'so' && git rev-parse HEAD` | **`error: src refspec so does not match any`**, exit 1 |\\n| 08:44 → now | item_12 … item_249 | \\\"Let me search the web for rust src refspec so does not match any.\\\" plus `codex_apps` MCP tool `search_service.web_run` with **`arguments: {}`** | **119 times** the same `InvalidActionArgumentsError: Provide either an input string or top-level Search commands, without combining them.` (`error_code INVALID_ACTION_ARGUMENTS`, `failure_stage argument_binding`) |\\n\\nAll of it is one Codex turn (`turn.started` once, never completed). The branch Hive Mind had checked out and named in its prompt was `issue-1-09b0c76bd0e4` (PR #2). The commit `feat: add main.rs` exists only in the task container; nothing was pushed. The issue also asks for a GitHub Actions workflow, which was never started.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\nCode references are at `v0.354.1`. Each item says whether `v0.355.0` changes it.\\n\\n**1. \\\"so\\\" is taken as the branch name** (`rust/src/agentic_coding/git_commit.rs:48-67`). `named_branch` walks word pairs of the whole request and returns the first word after a `git_branch_cue` surface (\\\"branch\\\") that passes `is_branch_name`. Hive Mind's Codex prompt says (`src/codex.prompts.lib.mjs:206`):\\n\\n> commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request **branch so** interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n\\nThat gives the pair `(\\\"branch\\\", \\\"so\\\")`. `\\\"so\\\"` is a legal ref name, so `CommitTarget::push_ref()` (`:32`) plans `git push -q origin 'so'`. The branch Hive Mind had already checked out (`issue-1-09b0c76bd0e4`) is never asked from git. *0.355.0 rewrites `named_branch` with `branch-binding-patterns` and a `branch-forbidden-substrings` policy, which should reject this word. It still reads the branch from prose, not from `git branch --show-current`.*\\n\\n**2. `web_run` is called with the wrong argument key** (`rust/src/agentic_coding/repair_loop.rs:741-746`). After the failed push, `repair_step` (the E149 / #1185 error-driven repair loop) plans a search for the error:\\n\\n```rust\\nif progress.search_output.is_none() {\\n    let Some(tool) = tool_for(tool_names, Capability::Search) else { … };\\n    let query = search_query(&failure.language, primary);\\n    return RepairOutcome::Search(plan_one(tool, json!({ \\\"query\\\": query }).to_string()));\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe key is always `\\\"query\\\"`, whatever the tool's JSON schema says. Codex's `codex_apps` / `search_service.web_run` takes `input`, so Codex binds nothing and sends `{}`. The tool answers `InvalidActionArgumentsError` (`failure_stage: argument_binding`). The search text itself was reasonable (\\\"rust src refspec so does not match any\\\"). *Unchanged in 0.355.0.*\\n\\n**3. Nothing ever stops the repeat.** Three guards exist, and none fires:\\n- **Search never counts as done.** `Progress::scan` (`progress.rs:157-163`) sets `search_output` only from a non-empty normalized payload of a `Capability::Search` result. The error result evidently never sets it (otherwise the next step would have been a fetch), so `repair_step:741` plans the search again on every turn.\\n- **The repair rung never advances.** `MAX_REPAIR_RUNGS = 3` (`repair_loop.rs:74`) bounds the repair ladder, but only a failed `Capability::Run` step advances a rung. A failed search does not, so the ladder stays at rung 0.\\n- **Identical failures are not counted.** `stop_repeated_failure` (`planner/steps.rs:48`) needs `identical_failures_of(tool) >= 2` (`progress.rs:431`), and `stop_repeated_call` (`steps.rs:17`) needs an earlier successful identical call. Neither condition was met in 119 identical failed calls; the most likely reason is that this MCP error result is not recorded as a failure (inference).\\n\\n*0.355.0 changes the evidence window of `stop_repeated_call` / `stop_repeated_failure` (`repetition_progress`) but not the search, rung or failure-classification paths.* This is the same family as #1154: a progress check that does not recognise the client's own tool result shape.\\n\\n**4. About 130 s of a full core per request** (inference from code reading; not profiled). On turn 1 the whole Hive Mind context is a single user message, so the chat \\\"prompt\\\" is the full 277 KB. `normalize_prompt` (`engine.rs:668`) lower-cases it, rewrites it character by character, re-joins the tokens and runs `expand_contractions` over every token. It is not memoized, and it runs again for each solver path (`solver.rs` around 460, 583, 656) and for each handler check (`rule_interpreter.rs:464` → `Context` at `:614`). That is roughly 20 full passes per request over a text that is mostly the same on every turn. A profile (`perf record -g -p <pid>` on the live sidecar) would confirm it. *Unchanged in 0.355.0.*\\n\\n## Cost\\n\\nEach Codex request takes Formal AI **121–144 s of 100% of one core**, from the first turn on:\\n\\n| Request | Body | Answer after |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 (08:29:05) | 277,529 B | 129.7 s |\\n| 6 (08:39:47) | 291,407 B | 123.4 s |\\n| 60 (10:35:14) | 338,320 B | 131.2 s |\\n| 124 (12:58:26) | 393,616 B | 143.5 s |\\n\\nThe body grows by about 864 bytes per looping turn: one more identical message and failed call. Codex sends the next request as soon as an answer arrives, so the core never idles. `formal-ai serve` had used 262 CPU-minutes in 267 minutes of life when it was sampled. On a 6-core host that also runs Claude and Codex tasks, the load average stayed at 6–9.\\n\\n## No logs were persisted\\n\\nThe sidecar's volume `/home/box/.formal-ai` was owned by root (a Hive Mind deployment problem, reported in link-assistant/hive-mind#3583), so every exchange logged three errors and nothing reached disk:\\n\\n- 381× `logging_failure` / `logging_failed_check_log_directory_permissions_and_space` (`reason file_write_failed`), the first at startup;\\n- about 124× `[dialog-log] failed to record exchange: Permission denied (os error 13)`;\\n- about 124× `[memory] failed to record live chat exchange: refusing to write an incompatible persisted-memory schema`. This is a misleading message for a directory that is empty and unwritable: there is no persisted schema to be incompatible with.\\n\\nFormal AI's defaults were already the most detailed: `--verbose` is the default, and stdout carries `debug` records. But the records about a request only say `large_native_diagnostic` with the body size and a SHA-256. So the planner's decisions (why `so`, why `web_run`, why `{}`, why the same plan again) are not visible anywhere, even at debug level.\\n\\nWhere the two messages come from:\\n\\n- `logging_failed_check_log_directory_permissions_and_space` is `structured_log.rs:317-331`. It is written once **per log event**, and the server goes on without logs. It never says which directory or which error (`Permission denied`).\\n- `refusing to write an incompatible persisted-memory schema` is `memory_sync.rs:432`. The store is marked `compatible = false` at `memory_sync.rs:274` when `synchronize_link_cli_projection()` fails. Here that is because the directory is not writable. The real error is printed **only when `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_DEBUG` is set** (`:268-272`), which is off by default. So the default log names a schema problem that does not exist and hides the actual cause.\\n\\n## Expected\\n\\n1. **Push to the current branch.** The push target comes from `git branch --show-current` (or `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`) in the working directory, or from the branch Hive Mind's prompt names, never from a word in prose. A push that fails with `src refspec … does not match any` is repaired by re-reading the current branch, not by a web search.\\n2. **Bind tool arguments from the tool's schema, or do not call the tool.** `web_run` (Codex's `codex_apps` `search_service.web_run`) needs an `input` string. A call whose required arguments come out empty is never sent: it is a planning error, logged with the schema and the values that could not be bound.\\n3. **Detect no progress.** The same tool, the same arguments and the same error N times in a row (3 is plenty) is a loop. Change strategy, or end the session with an honest failure report that names the loop. The tool error text here (`Provide either an input string…`) is a direct instruction that the planner ignored 119 times.\\n4. **Bound the cost per request.** About 130 s of a full core for a 277 KB request, before any history, makes every coding session take hours. Most of the request is Codex's fixed instructions and tool definitions, which are the same on every turn and can be parsed or indexed once per session.\\n5. **Fail loudly when logs cannot be written.** At startup, if `FORMAL_AI_LOG_DIR` / `~/.formal-ai` is not writable, say so once, clearly, and expose it on `/health` (for example `\\\"logs\\\": \\\"unwritable: Permission denied\\\"`), so the host (Hive Mind) can warn or fix it instead of 381 identical records. Name the real cause in the memory error, and print the `synchronize_link_cli_projection` error by default instead of only under `FORMAL_AI_MEMORY_DEBUG`.\\n6. **Log the planner's decisions at debug level** (chosen action, tool, bound arguments, the progress or loop state), not only the body size and hash, so a loop like this can be explained from the log alone (#1220).\\n\\n## Logs (private gists; the email and Codex `account_id` are redacted)\\n\\n- Task log (Codex event stream, start-command, solve), 2026-10-20 08:27 → 13:03 UTC: https://gist.github.com/konard/c24e69645c4fd35e3b6f5b73e6fda4e1\\n- Formal AI sidecar stdout (`docker logs --timestamps hive-mind-formal-ai`), 08:27 → 13:00 UTC: https://gist.github.com/konard/59ce995cffb9a10165fbece3e60e4a9c\\n\\n## Versions\\n\\nFormal AI 0.354.1 (sidecar image `konard/hive-mind-dind:2.39.2`, `formal-ai serve --agent-mode --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080`; latest release is 0.355.0), Codex CLI 0.163.0 (`codex exec`, `--model formal-ai`), Hive Mind 2.39.2. Codex conversation `01a1586d-a94a-7770-877b-2daebf8de380`, start-command execution `b8b78e59-8c3a-463d-a53b-3b274209a064`.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #1162 (E118, Hello World via Hive Mind and Codex; this is one more failure of it), #1154 (E119, Codex re-plans the same read 78 times: the same family of no-progress loop), #1185 (E149, error-driven repair loop: here the \\\"search the error\\\" step runs, with no arguments and no stop), #1189 (words from prose taken as targets: \\\"e.g.\\\" as a file, here \\\"so\\\" as a branch), #1211 (files named after words in the prompt), #1220 (debug logs by default).\\n- link-assistant/hive-mind#3048 (no default CPU limit for task containers; the sidecar has none either), link-assistant/hive-mind#2316 (the repeated-tool-call breaker exists only for Claude).\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_8d85cc77edc6b68a\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"mkdir -p -- .formal-ai && (lock='.formal-ai/general-change-plan.lino.lock'; tries=0; until mkdir -- \\\\\\\"$lock\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null; do tries=$((tries + 1)); [ \\\\\\\"$tries\\\\\\\" -lt 40 ] || exit 75; sleep 0.025; done; cleanup(){ rmdir -- \\\\\\\"$lock\\\\\\\"; }; trap cleanup EXIT; if [ -f '.formal-ai/general-change-plan.lino' ]; then grep -Fqx -- '  id \\\\\\\"general_change_plan_305b4fe18c5f6063\\\\\\\"' '.formal-ai/general-change-plan.lino'; status=$?; [ \\\\\\\"$status\\\\\\\" -le 1 ] || exit \\\\\\\"$status\\\\\\\"; else status=1; fi; if [ \\\\\\\"$status\\\\\\\" -eq 1 ]; then printf \\\\\\\"\\\\n%s\\\\\\\" 'general_change_plan\\\\n  id \\\\\\\"general_change_plan_305b4fe18c5f6063\\\\\\\"\\\\n  execution_mode \\\\\\\"command_output\\\\\\\"\\\\n  terminal_state \\\\\\\"executed\\\\\\\"\\\\n  goal \\\\\\\"0.354.1 loops for 4.5 h at 100% CPU via Codex: pushes to branch '\\\\\\\\''so'\\\\\\\\'' (a word from the prompt), then calls web_run with {} 119 times (wrong '\\\\\\\\''query'\\\\\\\\'' key, no repeat stop)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## What happened\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOn 2026-10-20 a Hive Mind run, `solve https://github.com/konard/test-hello-world-019fb331-c107-78c7-8ff6-9f127a3c593c/issues/1 --tool codex --model formal-ai`, kept the Formal AI sidecar at **100% of one CPU core for more than 4.5 hours** and produced nothing. It is still running; it was not stopped, so the loop can be watched live.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFormal AI did the real work in the first 13 minutes, then spent hours doing nothing useful:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Time (UTC) | Codex item | What Formal AI did | Result |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| 08:29 | item_1 | `gh issue view … --json title`, `… --json body` | ok |\\\\\\\\n| 08:31–08:33 | item_3, item_5 | added `main.rs`, `tests/verify-output.sh` | ok |\\\\\\\\n| 08:35 | item_7 | `rustc main.rs -o main` | exit 0 |\\\\\\\\n| 08:37 | item_9 | `sh tests/verify-output.sh` | `Hello, World!` |\\\\\\\\n| 08:41:51 | item_11 | `git add … && git commit … && git push -q origin '\\\\\\\\''so'\\\\\\\\'' && git rev-parse HEAD` | **`erro